      "gap": "The gate reviews the template. It does not watch a running account.",
      "id": "operational-excellence",
      "missing": [],
      "ok": true,
      "question": "How do you know a platform change is the one you reviewed?",
      "summary": "The synth is deterministic. CI diffs it against the fixture and runs the named invariant gate. A security group opened to the world fails no-world-cidrs and names the resource.",
      "title": "Operational excellence"
    },
    {
      "evidence": [
        "security/TenantKeyNorthwind",
        "security/BedrockRuntimeEndpoint",
        "security/BedrockPrivateZone",
        "security/Guardrail"
      ],
      "gap": "IAM cannot prove the task called ApplyGuardrail. The endpoint policy limits who may call.",
      "id": "security",
      "missing": [],
      "ok": true,
      "question": "What is allowed to reach a model, and with whose key?",
      "summary": "Workload subnets have no internet route. Only the Aegis task role is on the Bedrock endpoint policy. Lumen and Switchboard deny bedrock:*. Each tenant bucket uses its own CMK, and the key refuses a call without that tenant's encryption context. The guardrail is a resource the task is pinned to.",
      "title": "Security"
    },
    {
      "evidence": [
        "security/AegisService",
        "network/TransitGateway"
      ],
      "gap": "The transit gateway is a hub. Losing it stops every cross-account call.",
      "id": "reliability",
      "missing": [],
      "ok": true,
      "question": "What happens to a single bad AZ, and what is still a single point?",
      "summary": "Each VPC has two private subnets and each service asks for two tasks. The model path does not depend on a NAT gateway. The transit gateway still is one control plane for every account-to-account packet.",
      "title": "Reliability"
    },
    {
      "evidence": [
        "switchboard/SwitchboardService",
        "security/BedrockRuntimeEndpoint"
      ],
      "gap": "The latency numbers for Bedrock and KMS in the report are projections.",
      "id": "performance",
      "missing": [],
      "ok": true,
      "question": "Where does the request spend time, and what did we refuse to guess?",
      "summary": "Switchboard still chooses the model. The Bedrock call stays on an interface endpoint in the security VPC, so it does not cross the public internet. This repo measures synth time and the local guardrail stub. It does not measure a live Bedrock round trip.",
      "title": "Performance efficiency"
    },
    {
      "evidence": [
        "finops/PlatformBudget",
        "security/Guardrail"
      ],
      "gap": "The prices are a frozen list, not the AWS Price List API and not an invoice.",
      "id": "cost",
      "missing": [],
      "ok": true,
      "question": "What does a thousand requests cost, and which line is the platform?",
      "summary": "Model mix is the large line. The guardrail is the next one, because it is priced per text unit, so the design scans the user turn and the output and not the retrieved context. Private endpoints cost more per day than one NAT gateway. The console shows that premium instead of folding it into a single savings number. A budget resource in the finops account is the ceiling, not the forecast.",
      "title": "Cost optimization"
    },
    {
      "evidence": [
        "switchboard/SwitchboardService",
        "security/AegisTaskDefinition"
      ],
      "gap": "No carbon number is claimed. Smaller models are a token reduction, not a footprint audit.",
      "id": "sustainability",
      "missing": [],
      "ok": true,
      "question": "Which requests never needed the largest model?",
      "summary": "The mix sends FAQ and cited answers to a Haiku-class model and pins confidential text to a local model. The agent step still uses a Sonnet-class model. That split is the sustainability control this platform actually has.",
      "title": "Sustainability"
